>From the Memoirs of Hippie-Hea-Gno-Fem-Neu...

Througout my life, I've always tried to be nice to people and never to
hurt anyone. It's what my parents would've wanted. So when I set off on
the great adventure, I put my scalpel away, descended into the darkness
and tried to be nice to the creatures. Honestly, I did try. But most of
the time I had to run away from the horrible little things. My pet dog
Sandy was very good at chasing them away though.

Some of the creatures were nice to me. The other gnomes for example, and
their dwarvish friends. They had a little mine which they showed me
around, and eventually I came to a nice gnomish town. The priest in the
temple wanted a donation, so, in my very generous nature I gave him some
money. He said that he'd 'rewarded my generousity', whatever that means.

I carried on exploring the mines, when suddenly Sandy turned into a White
Dragon. I was scared for a moment, but he still seemed friendly, and I'd
found a whistle which seemed to keep him under control. Unfortunately, one
time when I wasn't paying attention, he slaughtered everything in the
gnomish town, except a light seller called Izchak, who sold me a lamp
which never goes out. I apologised to Hermes for not looking after Sandy
properly, then I thought I'd try on the dead guard's armour. Some of it
was really nice, and I ended up feeling much tougher than when I'd entered
the mines.

About now I found a horn which I tried to play. It didn't make a very nice
sound, but a blast of cold came out of it, killing a newt which happened
to be watching my performance. For a moment I was disgusted with myself...
I had always looked after animals and would never hurt them. But then, I
realised that killing things was fun.

So I took my scalpel out again, left the gnomish mines and started
killing. I still had Sandy in case I got into trouble. Soon I came across
a big room full of creatures containing lots of gold, and at the other end
a very shiny medallion which I decided to wear.

Throughout my time in the dungeon I'd found various spellbooks, which I
started to study from. One of the most useful spells helped me to identify
the junk which I'd picked up. Although I couldn't cast it very well, this
spell was very useful for the rest of my adventure.

One day I recieved a faint message from my homeland - they needed my help.
I found a portal which took me there, and flying Sandy over the water I
went to see my childhood mentor. He told me that an evil Cyclops was
making everything ill, and I had to stop him. So I proceeded to his lair,
keeping away from the water lest I should drown, and eventually zapped him
with a wand of the most concentrated energy, which killed him instantly. I
picked up his weapon, the Staff of Aesculapius, and a bell he'd left
behind, and took them back to my homeland.

I decided that from now on I should visit home very often to see my
friends, so I left all the stuff I wouldn't need right away there, and
then said my farewells and returned to the dungeon.

This part of the adventure is rather hard to remember, but I know I killed
a grey dragon at some point, and managed to harden its scales to make a
good piece of armour. I also remember trying to withdraw money from a
bank, but for some reason the staff there weren't very pleased to see me.
Eventually I reached the stronghold of the mythical Medusa, whom it would
seem, isn't that mythical. I was warned by a friendly Gnome-Lord about the
location of her lair, so I prepared myself - after flying one of my
dragons over the water (I had a small army of dragons now) I put on my
blindfold and paid her a visit. But she wasn't there. I took of my
blindfold and looked around... there were many statues, including one of
Perseus and one of a Gorgon named Medusa. Maybe she is just a myth after
all.

I smashed some of the statues to get myself a bag, which I'd been needing
for a long time. I then continued to explore, until I found a castle. The
inhabitants weren't too pleased to see me, so I had a long fight with
them, losing one of the bravest dragons I'd had in the progress.
Eventually all the creaturse were dead, and in one of the rooms I found a
wand, which my identifying spell said would give me wishes. "Great!" I
thought, and put it in my bag for emergencies.

I now indulged in some sorcery which the locals here call "Polypiling". I
put all the jumk which I didn't want in piles, and using a wand I'd fonud,
turned it into other stuff. This way I aquired several useful items,
including some medallions which would save my life, a ring which caused
conflict, a ring which stopped me being paralysed, a ring which made me
eat less, a ring which made me fly, another bag, and many maps of the
dungeon. The new bag was interesting, it seemed to make everything in it
weight much less. Very useful. I also used some of the rings I'd found to
turn myself into a cockatrice, and laid some eggs, for I'd heard that they
make very good weapons.

I then entered the depths of Hell. I fought many demons, some had stupid
names, some didn't, and explored many mazes (the maps I'd made really
helped). On my way down I killed a vampire who had an expensive looking
Candleabrum, and a Wizard with a dangerous spellbook. During this part of
my journey I had a dream, telling me of a vibrating square, and an
invocation. Using this as a guide, I completed a short ritual and entered
Moloch's Sanctum. I fought my way through the crowds of devil worshippers,
and entered his temple. The priest wasn't pleased to see me, so I killed
him, grabbed the amulet, as my dreams had told me to, and rose up through
the ceiling in style.

Now the real fight began... I was trying to go up, yet a mysterious force
kept pulling me down. I lost count of the number of times, but it was a
lot. Eventually, afater several days and nights, I fought my way back up
to the castle, and out of hell.

I kept going up, stopped off to say goodbye to my Mentor and pick up some
necessities from home, checked that I still knew all my spells and had
everything I might need, then ascended out of the dungeon. But Hermes had
more work for me yet. I found myself surrounded by earth. Using some wands
I'd brought along, I dug my way to a portal which took me to a place high
in the clouds. I flew towards the next portal, which was a place full of
fire and lava. I then flew over the lava, and into the sea (luckily I was
in an air bubble). For a long time I waited, but eventually I found a
portal in my bubble, which took me to the Astral Plane.

This was another place I'd imagined in my dreams. It contained the high
altars of three Gods. I knew that I had to find the altar of Hermes. My
heart was pounding as I took my first act on that plane... take several
wands of teleportion and a cockatrice egg out of my bag. The egg was
thrown to a nearby Arch-Lich, who caught it and turned to stone. Then, I
put on my ring which caused conflict, and started to fight my way through
the crowds. The wands of teleportion were useful to clear a path, as were
the wands of death I had. I killed Famine three times as I tried to get to
the nearest altar. I knew that the other Riders of the Apocalypse were
nearby as well, however thankfully I didn't have to fight them, as the
first altar belonged to Hermes.

I sacrificed the Amulet I'd picked up, and Hermes made me a demigod. It's
quite cool being immortal, but when I look around the demigod bar I still
feel pretty nervous. I know I've achieved something great (I never used
the the wishing wand I found, but it's great for stirring drinks). However
you know that some of the people here could do that without eating, or
praying, or being able to read or write. That's pretty amazing. But then
again... if I couldn't read or write, I wouldn't be able to write this
account of my travels.


